WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.080
The cerebral cortex needs two kinds of neuron.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Some excite their neighbours.

3
00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:09.980
Others inhibit them.

4
00:00:10.883 --> 00:00:15.843
In rodents, roughly a fifth of cortical neurons are inhibitory interneurons.

5
00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:20.143
Yet the cortex's own wall makes the excitatory kind.

6
00:00:20.983 --> 00:00:25.823
These pyramidal neurons climb straight out along radial glial fibres, long struts

7
00:00:25.823 --> 00:00:26.823
that span the wall.

8
00:00:27.617 --> 00:00:30.057
So where do the inhibitory neurons come from?

9
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.980
Start with a map. The dorsal part of the embryonic forebrain, the pallium, forms the

10
00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:42.980
cortex.

11
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:48.090
Its ventral part, the subpallium, bulges into the ventricle as three swellings.

12
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:53.050
These are the lateral, medial and caudal ganglionic eminences.

13
00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.810
Despite the name, they are not ganglia.

14
00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:00.730
They are temporary germinal zones, packed with dividing progenitors.

15
00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:05.103
Pallial progenitors make neurons that release glutamate.

16
00:01:06.123 --> 00:01:10.483
Subpallial progenitors make neurons that release GABA, the main inhibitory

17
00:01:10.483 --> 00:01:11.483
transmitter.

18
00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:17.013
In rodents, most cortical interneurons are born in the medial and caudal eminences.

19
00:01:18.053 --> 00:01:19.953
Then they travel sideways into the cortex.

20
00:01:21.083 --> 00:01:25.123
Moving parallel to the brain surface is called tangential migration.

21
00:01:29.617 --> 00:01:31.977
Here is the idea to carry through this lesson.

22
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.480
Born outside the cortex, steered by what the cell can read.

23
00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.830
At the turns studied so far, the road holds a cue.

24
00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.770
The travelling cell carries what answers it.

25
00:01:48.683 --> 00:01:51.483
So a fault can sit in the cell, which cannot answer.

26
00:01:52.283 --> 00:01:54.503
Or it can sit in the road, which lacks the cue.

27
00:01:55.517 --> 00:01:59.237
Swapping tissue between normal and mutant animals tells you which.

28
00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:04.610
And in the cortex, the lane's chemokine signal helps keep a cell in the lane.

29
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.760
Hold on to that. The next chapters test it.

30
00:02:09.833 --> 00:02:13.733
One limit first. These results come from mice and rats.

31
00:02:14.783 --> 00:02:18.403
In humans, the eminences are seen as the main source too.

32
00:02:19.183 --> 00:02:22.443
But cortical progenitors also make some inhibitory neurons.

33
00:02:23.516 --> 00:02:26.636
Most cortical interneurons start outside the cortex.

34
00:02:27.556 --> 00:02:29.516
What they can read helps decide where they go.
